afpconnection: keep a request until it is sent, and its hash under the lock

GVfs's AFP backend (1.60.3, and its main branch on 2026-10-23) dies now
and then (gvfsd-afp, SIGSEGV or SIGABRT), its mount gone, two ways:

- A request is freed when its reply is handled (the request hash's
  value-free function) while the callback of its last write may still
  be to run: a write that completes within the main-loop pass that
  started it returns through an idle, and a server that answers at once
  (one on the same machine, a fast LAN) has its reply read first.
  write_command_cb then reads the freed request. Fedora's retrace server
  holds such crashes in write_command_cb.
- The worker thread never makes its context the thread-default, so the
  connection's read and write callbacks run in the main thread's, which
  looks requests up in the hash and removes them without the lock the
  worker holds to insert them: the hash breaks (g_hash_table_remove's
  assertion that it holds nodes).

A request is now counted: one reference while it is sent, one while it
waits in the hash, which is read and changed under the connection's lock
everywhere. A write that fails also stops freeing its request twice (the
hash's remove, then free_request_data), and ends the request only if its
reply has not.

Applied by tools/test/gvfs-nfs-afp.sh to the GVfs it runs, until upstream
has it. Diff against gvfs-1.60.3, daemon/gvfsafpconnection.c.

